// synth_proposer.mn — Hμ.synth-proposer peer handle
//
// The oracle's exploration substrate. Replaces the DELETED OneShot
// enumeration stub (it returned []) with real MultiShot
// candidate enumeration over the constraint space. This is what
// makes `??` THE developer's primary write-mode verb (SYNTAX §Partial
// application — one absence marker across space and time): with this
// handler installed, every `??` position invokes
// the proposer chain; the gradient narrows the candidate space; the
// surviving candidate IS the synthesized residue.
//
// Per PLAN §2's cached cursor: the oracle's runtime IS IC + Pure-
// over-broader-input + the multi-shot resume primitive composed.
// This file is that composition; the kernel does the work.
//
// Anchor 0 dream code. Wheel canonical IS the ULTIMATE FORM.
// Verification by simulation, walkthrough, audit. lux3.wasm is not
// the arbiter. The seed transcribes after first-light-L1 + H7
// MultiShot emit per Tier 3 growth pattern.
//
// Anchor 4 — build the wheel; never wrap the axle. The proposer
// chain (enumerative / smt / llm-handler / user-fed) is FOUR peer
// handlers on the SAME Synth effect; swap the chain, swap the
// proposer; the verification stays in the kernel.
//
// Composition tally — every read is from substrate that exists today:
//
//   Synth effect (3 ops; OneShot/MultiShot mix)  — src/mentl.mn:89-93
//   AnnotationSuggestion / Cursor                — src/types.mn (Hμ.cursor)
//   graph_push_checkpoint / graph_rollback        — src/graph.mn + types.mn
//   verify_after_apply (the proof gate)           — src/mentl.mn:277-286
//   Reason chain (per-candidate provenance)       — src/types.mn Reason ADT
//   MultiShot resume discipline                   — PLAN §2's cached cursor
//   Pure-over-broader-input enumeration           — primitive #4 row algebra
//
// Drift modes refused per edit site:
//
//   Drift 1 (vtable):       Synth handler chain via ~> composition;
//                           no fn-pointer table for proposer dispatch.
//   Drift 2 (env frame):    Candidate state is the proposer's
//                           handler-state, NOT a parallel enumeration
//                           frame stack.
//   Drift 4 (MTL):          ONE Synth effect + multiple peer handlers
//                           on it; not stacked single-concern wrappers.
//   Drift 6 (special):      Each candidate (Empty/SuggestEffectRow/
//                           Constructor-fill/Lambda-shape) goes through
//                           the SAME `candidate_proven` gate; no special-
//                           case for "easy" cases.
//   Drift 7 (parallel arr): Candidate is one record-shape with Node +
//                           Reason; not parallel (asts[], reasons[]).
//   Drift 8 (string flag):  ProposerKind is a tagged ADT.
//   Drift 9 (deferred):     The chain is named in full; each proposer
//                           bodied; BOTH arms live — propose (the
//                           OneShot survivor-set) and
//                           enumerate_inhabitants (the multi-shot
//                           resume-per-proven fan, gated by its own
//                           frontier fixture; the fork pair =
//                           graph checkpoint + heap region).
import types

import effects

import graph

import own

import verify

import mentl

import parser

import infer

import lower

import egraph

import cursor

import query

// ─── ProposerKind — which proposer in the chain emitted a candidate ─
type ProposerKind
  // type-directed enumeration over the constraint space
  = ProposerEnumerative
  // the in-scope vocabulary, searched outward from the hole's module, and
  // the ring the name was found in
  | ProposerVocabulary(VocabRing)
  // SMT solver via Verify-handler-swap (Arc F.1)
  | ProposerSMT
  // the handler catalog: an install proposed at a `?? ~> h` hole, named and
  // not yet built. A wrap changes what the body means, so it is a fill
  // judged here when it lands, never an annotation the gradient offers
  | ProposerHandlerCatalog
  // seeds the developer supplies at the position
  | ProposerUser

// Where the vocabulary search found a name, nearest first. The hole's own
// module is what the developer wrote; an authored import is vocabulary they
// chose to reach; the rest of the link — the pre-imported prelude and the
// runtime floor under it — is in scope by name only because the link
// flattens every module into one env.
type VocabRing = OwnModule | AuthoredImport | Linked

// ─── EnrichedCandidate — Candidate + provenance ────────────────────
//
// `synth_propose` answers with one Proposal; this file's chain carries
// each candidate node with its proposer of origin until then. Drift 7 closure: ONE
// record-shape, never parallel arrays. RANK IS A PROJECTION, NOT A
// FIELD (Hβ.synth.rank-is-a-projection-not-a-field, resolved): the
// stored cost field compared a measured proximity against invented
// enumerator constants (a vocabulary call genuinely ranking 0.25 lost
// to a literal's made-up 0.3) and rode stale when extraction swapped
// the node — `rank_of` now reads the LIVE node at sort time, once per
// candidate, through the same env/refs reads the gradient owns.
type EnrichedCandidate
  = EnrichedCandidate({node: Node, origin: ProposerKind, reason: Reason, writes: [(Int, ContextFact)]})

// WHAT A CANDIDATE TAUGHT THE CONTEXT (C6): the context cells its segment
// bound, each with the meaning the cell held before the rollback undid it —
// a type chased deep, a row resolved. Empty until the candidate is judged;
// a proven survivor is sealed with its segment's writes, and the divergence
// between two survivors is read here first.
type ContextFact = FactTy(Ty) | FactRow(EffRow)

// One candidate's verdict from its segment: proven or not, the Reason the
// verdict carries (the candidate's own when proven, the medium's refusal
// otherwise), and the context writes the segment carried out.
type SegmentVerdict = SegmentVerdict({proven: Bool, reason: Reason, writes: [(Int, ContextFact)]})

// AST fragment
//
// why this was proposed
//
// which proposer emitted
//
// ─── synth_default — the canonical proposer chain entry point ──────
//
// Composes the four proposer handlers in priority order:
//
//   user-fed seeds (rank 1.0) · the handler catalog (0.9) ·
//   refinement-aware SMT (0.7) · type-directed enumeration (0.5)
//   — each a `~>`-installed Synth peer, named, post-first-light.
//
// Each proposer's output is gated by `candidate_proven` inside the segment
// walk; only proven candidates reach the parent. The chain composes via
// `~>` per primitive #2 (handlers); same kernel; one effect.
//
// Per PLAN §1: dead variants WIDEN the
// constraint space at proof-search time, NOT teach Mentl wrong
// vocabulary. Each proposer emits CANDIDATES; the kernel's
// verify_after_apply is the proof gate.
handler synth_default {
  synth_propose(allowed_effects, context) => {
    // Every candidate is minted INTO THE HOLE'S MODULE. The judgment leaves
    // whichever module it entered last in force, so a candidate born after
    // it read as that stranger's: its diagnostics landed at `strings:0:0`,
    // and once the import gate read module identity every same-module
    // reference was refused as a cross-module one (F0b's first frontier
    // run: twelve legs, one root). The hole's column is the fact.
    graph_module_enter(graph_module_of(context_hole_handle(context)))
    // The TARGET is read from the hole's own cell, once, at entry — the
    // Context carries the handle and the type is one dereference away.
    // Threading it locally is a binding; carrying it across the op
    // boundary was a copy.
    match context_target_ty(context) {
      target_ty => // The in-scope VOCABULARY first (PLAN §1's second moat: type-directed
      // search over what is already in scope, pruned by the row at every
      // step), then the shape enumerators; every candidate through the ONE
      // proof gate. Row-rejected vocabulary keeps its Reasons.
      // The candidate space's own frontier, read BEFORE any enumeration
      // mints into it: the form half saturates exactly the range this propose
      // minted (PLAN §5's fork/merge duality — the range is what keeps merging
      // cheap at a cursor position).
      match graph_next() {
        cand_start => match enumerate_vocabulary_calls(
          target_ty,
          allowed_effects,
          context,
          vocabulary_rings(context_hole_handle(context))
        ) {
          (vocab, row_rejected) => match search_outward(vocab, row_rejected, context_hole_handle(context), cand_start) {
            (found, vocab_refused) => match verify_each_enriched(
              enumerate_typed(target_ty, allowed_effects, context),
              context_hole_handle(context),
              cand_start
            ) {
              (shaped, shape_refused) => {
                let survivors =
                  concat_candidates(found, shaped) |> collapse_equivalent |> rank_sort(context_hole_handle(context)) |> reference_before_mint
                // Both refusal channels, one list: the ROW pruned a candidate
                // before it was ever judged, the CELL refused one that was.
                // A developer auditing the proposal sees every name the medium
                // considered and why each is not here — in the rings the
                // search reached, never the whole link.
                resume(Proposal({rejected: vocab_refused ++ shape_refused, verdict: verdict_of(survivors, target_ty, context)}))
              },
            },
          },
        },
      },
    }
  },
}

// ─── enumerate_typed — type-directed candidate enumeration ──────────
//
// The core of the enumerative proposer. Per the gradient discipline
// (PLAN §2's cached cursor): the candidate space is bounded by the
// target type + allowed-effects constraint. Each candidate is one
// way to inhabit `target_ty` under `allowed_effects`.
//
// Strategy:
//
//   1. If target is a TName (ADT), enumerate constructors.
//   2. If target is a TFun, propose lambda shapes.
//   3. If target is TInt/TFloat/String/TUnit, propose literals.
//   4. If target is TVar (unbound), propose ?? (nested hole).
//
// Each strategy yields a small set; verify_each_enriched discards
// candidates whose tentative apply doesn't unify under the
// allowed_effects row.
fn enumerate_typed(target_ty, allowed_effects, context) = match target_ty {
  TName(name, args) => enumerate_constructors(name, args, context),
  TFun(params, ret, row) => enumerate_lambdas(params, ret, row, context),
  TInt => enumerate_int_literals(context),
  TByte => enumerate_int_literals(context),
  TFloat => enumerate_float_literals(context),
  TUnit => one_unit_candidate(context),
  TVar(_) => one_hole_candidate(context),
  TRecord(fields) => enumerate_records(fields, context),
  TRecordOpen(fields, rest) => enumerate_records(fields, context),
  TRefined(_, _) => // The refinement is the strongest thing known about the hole, and this arm
  // used to recurse on the BASE and drop it — the predicate sitting one field
  // away while three integer seeds were minted for a type that admits one.
  // The DOMAIN is that predicate read as inhabitants (verify.mn), so the seeds
  // come FROM it and the ones it excludes are never constructed. The post-hoc
  // gate stays: a domain over-approximates by contract, so it decides what to
  // build and `refinement_admits` still decides what survives.
  enumerate_domain(target_ty, allowed_effects, context),
  TAlias(_, inner) => enumerate_typed(inner, allowed_effects, context),
  TReprPin(_, base) => enumerate_typed(base, allowed_effects, context),
  TCont(_, _, _, _) => one_continuation_candidate(context),
  TList(elem) => // A byte-element list IS String (the canonical alias's inner) — a
  // String-typed hole proposes string literals, never list literals.
  match seq_elem_face(elem) {
    TByte => enumerate_string_literals(context),
    _ => enumerate_list_candidates(elem, context),
  },
  TTuple(elems) => enumerate_tuple_candidates(elems, context),
}

// The Intent-Boundary pin grounding the candidate concat: the vocabulary
// list is a fold's tuple component, whose element type the graph cannot
// prove at the ++ site (the [LowExpr] precedent — the author states the
// element identity once, both operands ground).
fn concat_candidates(a: [EnrichedCandidate], b: [EnrichedCandidate]) -> [EnrichedCandidate] = a ++ b

// ─── The vocabulary enumerator — the finite in-scope call space ──────
//
// Every in-scope fn that could fill the hole is a candidate: a
// ZERO-ARGUMENT fn as a call whose return fits the target, and at a
// FUNCTION-typed hole a fn of the target's shape as a REFERENCE (SYNTAX
// §«Function literals»: a function value is reached by reference, or
// minted). The ALLOWED row — what the enclosing declaration may perform at
// the hole (`allowed_row_at`) — gates each through the crown's own
// subsumption; a reference's row is charged where the stage applies it, so
// the same gate judges it. Three outcomes per name, each tagged with the
// ring the name was found in:
//   admitted  — a node, minted with its Why, into the proof gate;
//   rejected  — the row refused it; the (name, Reason) pair SURVIVES to
//               the projection (the rejection teaches);
//   excluded  — its free-name closure reaches the enclosing decl:
//               proposing choose() inside choose, or main() where main
//               calls choose, is nontermination by construction (silent —
//               a structural impossibility, not a teaching moment).
// Calls that take arguments are the recursive-hole general form
// (Hβ.synth.vocabulary-arg-holes); the reach walk's O(1) form is the
// call-edge index (Hβ.synth.vocabulary-reach-index).
fn enumerate_vocabulary_calls(target_ty, allowed_row, context, rings) with !Mutate = match context {
  Context(scope_bindings, _active, enclosing, _hole_h) => fold(
    ([], []),
    (acc, entry) => match acc {
      (accepted, rejected) => match entry {
        (name, scheme, reason, kind) => match kind {
          FnScheme | ValueScheme => // A value binding of function type is vocabulary exactly as a
          // declared fn is: a reference to it fills a function-typed hole.
          match judgment_ty(scheme) {
            TFun(params, ret, row) => if len(params) == 0 && heads_may_unify(ret, target_ty) && !reaches_decl(name, enclosing, []) {
              if row_subsumes(row, allowed_row) {
                // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by the in-scope zero-arg vocabulary, not a loop accumulator
                (accepted ++ [vocabulary_call_candidate(name, row, allowed_row, ring_of_name(rings, name))], rejected)
              } else {
                (
                  accepted,
                  // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by the in-scope zero-arg vocabulary, not a loop accumulator
                  rejected ++ [(name, Inferred("performs {show_effrow(row)} — refused by the target row {show_effrow(allowed_row)}"), ring_of_name(rings, name))]
                )
              }
            } else if fn_heads_may_unify(params, ret, target_ty) && !reaches_decl(name, enclosing, []) {
              // A FUNCTION-typed hole is filled first by a REFERENCE to a
              // function already in scope. The reference's row is charged
              // where the stage applies, so the same row gate judges it.
              if row_subsumes(row, allowed_row) {
                // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by the in-scope vocabulary of one arity, not a loop accumulator
                (accepted ++ [vocabulary_ref_candidate(name, row, allowed_row, ring_of_name(rings, name))], rejected)
              } else {
                (
                  accepted,
                  // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by the in-scope vocabulary of one arity, not a loop accumulator
                  rejected ++ [(name, Inferred("performs {show_effrow(row)} when applied — refused by the target row {show_effrow(allowed_row)}"), ring_of_name(rings, name))]
                )
              }
            } else {
              (accepted, rejected)
            },
            _ => (accepted, rejected),
          },
          _ => (accepted, rejected),
        },
      },
    },
    scope_bindings
  ),
}

// ─── The vocabulary is searched OUTWARD from the hole ─────────────────
//
// PLAN §1: the medium proposes by GUIDED search, proximity among its
// priors, pruned by proof at every step. The search judges the hole's own
// module first and stops at the first ring that proves a survivor; a
// farther ring is judged only when every nearer one proved nothing, and its
// refusals are listed only then, because a name the search never reached
// is not part of this answer. Measured 2026-09-25 at `5 |> ??` in
// tests/frontier/mn-pipe-stage-fill.mn, whose one function sits above the
// hole: the whole link was one ring, so `decode_stride`, `hex_val`,
// `escape_len`, `escape_tail`, `hash` and `id` — reachable from the program
// only because the link flattens every module into one env — tied with it,
// and the hole could never fill.
fn search_outward(vocab, row_rejected, hole_h, cand_start) =
  [OwnModule, AuthoredImport, Linked] |> fold(([], []), search_ring(vocab, row_rejected, hole_h, cand_start))

fn search_ring(vocab, row_rejected, hole_h, cand_start, acc, ring) = match acc {
  (found, refused) => if len(found) > 0 {
    acc
  } else {
    match verify_each_enriched(vocab |> filter(found_in(ring)), hole_h, cand_start) {
      (verified, cell_refused) => (verified, refused ++ (row_rejected |> filter(refused_in(ring)) |> map(refusal_words)) ++ cell_refused),
    }
  },
}

fn found_in(ring, c) with Pure = match c {
  EnrichedCandidate(cr) => match cr.origin {
    ProposerVocabulary(r) => r == ring,
    _ => false,
  },
}

// `ring: VocabRing` is the Intent Boundary carrying the eq PROOF: the
// refusal is a bare triple, so without it `r == ring` compared a type
// variable at emit and fell to a one-word compare.
fn refused_in(ring: VocabRing, refusal) with Pure = match refusal {
  (_, _, r) => r == ring,
}

fn refusal_words(refusal) = match refusal {
  (name, reason, _) => (name, reason),
}

// The ring a name is found in: declared by the hole's own module, by a
// module it reaches through an AUTHORED import, or only by the link.
fn ring_of_name(rings, name: String) = if any({ n => n == name }, rings.home) {
  OwnModule
} else if any({ n => n == name }, rings.imported) {
  AuthoredImport
} else {
  Linked
}

// The rings, read once per proposal from the hole's own module node: the
// names its decls declare, and the names declared by every module it
// reaches through authored import edges. The driver PRE-imports the
// prelude rather than drawing an edge anyone wrote, so the prelude and the
// runtime floor under it stay in the outer ring unless the program imports
// them by name.
//
// The membership is read BY NAME because the env entry the vocabulary
// enumerates carries no edge to its decl: a judged fn is published as
// `Frozen(qs, ty)` by `generalize`, so the handle `graph_module_of` would
// read is gone by the time anything enumerates the env (PLAN §7, "Schemes
// are VALUES, not edges"). When the binding keeps its decl handle, the ring
// is one column read and these name lists are deleted.
fn vocabulary_rings(hole_h) = match graph_node_body(graph_module_of(hole_h)) {
  NModule(m) => {
    let cells = module_cells()
    let reached = authored_reach(cells, import_paths_of_decls(m.decls), [])
    {
      home: map(stmt_decl_name, m.decls),
      imported: cells |> filter(cell_reached(reached)) |> map(cell_decl_names) |> flatten,
    }
  },
  _ => {
    // The module column is written only with NModule handles, so a hole
    // whose module is anything else was minted outside every module — a
    // defect in the caller, which traps rather than answering with an
    // empty vocabulary nobody could tell from a real one.
    load_i32(0 - 1)
    {home: [], imported: []}
  },
}

// The module paths the hole's module reaches through authored import edges,
// read from the cells' own decls so the walk never rescans the weave.
fn authored_reach(cells, frontier, seen) = if len(frontier) == 0 {
  seen
} else if contains_path(seen, last(frontier)) {
  authored_reach(cells, drop_last(frontier), seen)
} else {
  authored_reach(
    cells,
    drop_last(frontier) ++ cell_imports(cells, last(frontier)),
    push(seen, last(frontier))
  )
}

fn cell_imports(cells, path: String) = fold([], cell_imports_pick(path), cells)

fn cell_imports_pick(path: String, acc, c) = match c {
  (_h, p, _s, d) => if p == path { import_paths_of_decls(d) } else { acc },
}

fn cell_reached(reached, c) = match c {
  (_h, p, _s, _d) => contains_path(reached, p),
}

fn cell_decl_names(c) = match c {
  (_h, _p, _s, d) => map(stmt_decl_name, d),
}

// Answers whether two types COULD unify, from their head constructors alone —
// the sound index that keeps the vocabulary from judging the whole prelude at
// every hole. It never rejects a unifiable pair, so it can hide no survivor.
//
// PROPAGATION PRECEDES ENUMERATION (PLAN §11.1), and this is the sound
// form of it at the vocabulary. The question a pre-filter may ask is not
// "are these the same type" — that is unification's, and asking it here is
// how the old matcher hid proven survivors — but "could these POSSIBLY
// unify", which is decided by the head constructors alone: two types can
// unify only if either side is still a variable, or their heads agree.
// Unification remains the DECIDER inside the segment; this is the index
// that keeps the medium from judging the whole prelude at every hole.
//
// Soundness is the whole property: this never rejects a unifiable pair, so
// no survivor can be hidden by it. `[a]` against `[Int]` passes (heads
// agree, the unify binds); `Option(a)` against `Option(Int)` passes; a
// refined alias peels to its base, so `pure_seven() -> Seven` reaches an
// `Int` hole and the refinement gate rules on the value — where the old
// name-equality rule refused it outright.
fn heads_may_unify(a, b) with Pure = match a {
  TVar(_) => true,
  TAlias(_, inner) => heads_may_unify(inner, b),
  TRefined(inner, _) => heads_may_unify(inner, b),
  _ => match b {
    TVar(_) => true,
    TAlias(_, inner) => heads_may_unify(a, inner),
    TRefined(inner, _) => heads_may_unify(a, inner),
    _ => same_head(a, b),
  },
}

fn same_head(a, b) with Pure = match a {
  TList(_) => match b {
    TList(_) => true,
    _ => false,
  },
  TName(an, _) => match b {
    TName(bn, _) => an == bn,
    _ => false,
  },
  TFun(_, _, _) => match b {
    TFun(_, _, _) => true,
    _ => false,
  },
  TTuple(_) => match b {
    TTuple(_) => true,
    _ => false,
  },
  TRecord(_) => match b {
    TRecord(_) => true,
    _ => false,
  },
  _ => same_ground(a, b),
}

// THE RETURN-TYPE EQUALITY MATCHER IS GONE, and what it was doing is the point.
// It compared a callee's declared return against the target by name and
// ground shape — a hand-rolled, weaker second copy of the union-find the
// medium already owns — and it was UNSOUND in the direction that costs the
// developer: a zero-arg `empty() -> [a]` at a `[Int]` hole is one type
// under unification and two shapes under a structural compare, so a proven
// survivor was never offered and the medium asked a question it could have
// answered. The row prune stays (it is a real Boolean fact, cheap and
// exact); type admission is now decided where it was always decided for
// every other expression in the language — by unifying the candidate's own
// cell with the hole's, inside the segment, with rollback. The proof
// becomes the dispatch.
fn vocabulary_call_candidate(name, row, allowed_row, ring) with !Mutate = EnrichedCandidate({
  node: synth_node(CallExpr(synth_node(VarRef(name)), [])),
  origin: ProposerVocabulary(ring),
  reason: Inferred("an in-scope call {name}() — row {show_effrow(row)} admitted by {show_effrow(allowed_row)}"),
  writes: [],
})

fn vocabulary_ref_candidate(name, row, allowed_row, ring) with !Mutate = EnrichedCandidate({
  node: synth_node(VarRef(name)),
  origin: ProposerVocabulary(ring),
  reason: Inferred("the in-scope function {name} — row {show_effrow(row)} admitted by {show_effrow(allowed_row)}"),
  writes: [],
})

// Could a function of this arity, parameters and return fill a
// function-typed hole? The sound index again: equal arity (a function type
// of another arity never unifies), and every parameter and the return
// head-compatible — never a rejected unifiable pair, and it keeps the
// prelude's whole function vocabulary from being judged, and listed as
// refused, at every stage hole. `same_head` alone treats every two function
// types alike, which is right for a head and too wide for a reference.
fn fn_heads_may_unify(params, ret, target_ty) = match target_ty {
  TFun(tparams, tret, _) => len(params) == len(tparams) && heads_may_unify(ret, tret) && all(params_heads_agree, zip(params, tparams)),
  TAlias(_, inner) => fn_heads_may_unify(params, ret, inner),
  _ => false,
}

fn params_heads_agree(pair) with Pure = match pair {
  (a, b) => heads_may_unify(param_ty(a), param_ty(b)),
}

// candidate_rank — the gradient reading LOCAL intent (PLAN §5's felt
// endpoint: the Reason chains carrying upstream why, proximity, the
// in-scope vocabulary). The rank is a SUM of graph reads, never a
// learned weight: the decl's nearness to the hole, plus every existing
// USE of the name weighted by its own nearness — vocabulary already
// flowing near the hole is the strongest prior; an unused far name
// sinks toward the base. The base 0.5 keeps ranked vocabulary level
// with the shape enumerators' tiers until a real signal lifts it. This
// is the field's FIRST reader — cost was a write-only literal before
// (the resume_kinds pattern: a score claiming gradient-ness that read
// nothing).
//
// Every term is the cursor's own proximity between two HANDLES — the hole,
// the name's declaration, each use — so a use in another file is as far as
// its module is from the hole's. Until 2026-10-01 the terms compared bare
// spans, and a reference at line 12 of any module read as maximally near a
// hole at line 12 (Hβ.synth.proximity-compares-across-modules). A name with
// no declaration node (a primitive) sits at the farthest tier: the gradient
// never reaches zero, and a position nothing places is never near.
fn candidate_rank(name: String, hole_h, reach, decls) with !Mutate = {
  let decl_near = match smap_get(decls, name) {
    Some(dh) => position_proximity(hole_h, dh, reach),
    None => cross_module_weight(),
  }
  let use_near =
    fold(0.0, (acc, h) => acc + position_proximity(hole_h, h, reach), ref_handles_of(name))
  0.5 + decl_near + use_near
}

// Every module's top-level declarations by the names they bind — a
// function or handler by its own name, a type's constructors and an
// effect's ops by theirs, each at its declaration's node — built once per
// proposal so a candidate's declaration is a handle the proximity reads.
// The env cannot answer this: a judged binding is published as a scheme
// VALUE with no edge back to its declaration
// (Hβ.synth.vocabulary-ring-is-read-by-name).
fn decl_index() with !Mutate = fold(smap_new(), decl_index_cell, module_cells())

fn decl_index_cell(acc, c) = match c {
  (_h, _p, _s, decls) => fold(acc, decl_index_node, decls),
}

fn decl_index_node(acc, d) = match d {
  N(body, _, h) => match body {
    NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)) => smap_add(acc, name, h),
    NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(hname, _, _, _, _, _)) => smap_add(acc, hname, h),
    NStmt(TypeDefStmt(_, variants)) => fold(
      acc,
      (a, v) => match v {
        (vname, _, _) => smap_add(a, vname, h),
      },
      variants
    ),
    NStmt(EffectDeclStmt(_, _, ops)) => fold(
      acc,
      (a, op) => match op {
        (oname, _, _, _) => smap_add(a, oname, h),
      },
      ops
    ),
    _ => acc,
  },
}

// rank_of — RANK IS A PROJECTION of the LIVE candidate (the resolved
// peer Hβ.synth.rank-is-a-projection-not-a-field): a named callee — a
// vocabulary or constructor call, one arm because a ctor call IS a
// CallExpr(VarRef) — ranks by the gradient's proximity read, its decl
// reason read live from the env; a nameless candidate (a lambda, a
// literal) carries the bare base with no invented differentiation (the
// stored form compared a measured proximity against enumerator
// constants, and a swapped e-class member rode a stale number).
// Computed ONCE per candidate at sort entry — the same refs walk the
// stored form paid at enrichment, moved to the read point.
fn rank_of(c, hole_h, reach, decls) with !Mutate = match c {
  EnrichedCandidate(cr) => match cr.node {
    N(body, _, _) => match body {
      NExpr(CallExpr(callee, _)) => match callee {
        N(cb, _, _) => match cb {
          NExpr(VarRef(name)) => candidate_rank(name, hole_h, reach, decls),
          _ => 0.5,
        },
      },
      NExpr(VarRef(name)) => // A reference ranks by the same local-intent read as a call of it.
      candidate_rank(name, hole_h, reach, decls),
      _ => 0.5,
    },
  },
}

fn context_hole_handle(context) with Pure = match context {
  Context(_, _, _, hole_h) => hole_h,
}

// The hole's type is a READ of its handle, never a field beside it: the
// live node kind answers at the moment it is asked, so it cannot describe
// a cell that has since moved (and the rank reads the handle itself, its
// module and its declaration, never a span copied out of it).
//
// Read RESOLVED: a hole's cell can be bound to a type whose parts are
// themselves cells the judgment has since bound, and the head index reads
// heads. Unresolved, the pipe stage in `5 |> ??` read `a -> b` though the
// graph had proved `Int -> Int`, so every one-parameter fn in the link
// passed the index and ~70 were judged or listed as refused at one hole
// (measured 2026-09-25) — the index answering from less than the graph knew.
//
// The cell holds a SHAPE (P0): a refinement is a fact a value carries, and
// a hole carries none, so what the hole DEMANDS is the contract a claim
// raised over it — a return, an argument, a let annotation (graph_claims_of,
// noted where the claim was raised). That contract is the target the
// enumerators read, refinement and all; a hole nothing claimed answers its
// shape.
fn context_target_ty(context) = hole_demand(context_hole_handle(context))

// What a hole demands, the one read the proposer's target and the address
// surface's Query line share: the contract claimed of it, else its cell.
fn hole_demand(hole_h) = match graph_claims_of(hole_h) {
  [claimed, ..._] => chase_deep(claimed),
  [] => match graph_chase(hole_h) {
    GNode(kind, _) => chase_deep(ty_of_kind(hole_h, kind)),
  },
}

// rank_sort — survivors ordered by rank, highest first: each candidate's
// rank projected once at entry, the insertion build keyed on the pair
// (the survivor set is small by construction — the in-scope zero-arg
// vocabulary plus the shape enumerators). The ORDER is the only thing
// rank decides; a constraint-tie still teaches (the render's collapse
// line is unchanged — rank is a prior, never a guess). The hole's module
// reach and the declarations' index are read once here, for every
// candidate the sort ranks.
fn rank_sort(hole_h, cands) with !Mutate = {
  let reach = module_reach_of(graph_module_of(hole_h))
  let decls = decl_index()
  cands |> map((c) => (rank_of(c, hole_h, reach, decls), c)) |> fold([], rank_insert) |> map({ (_, c) => c })
}

fn rank_insert(sorted, rc) = match sorted {
  [] => [rc],
  [h, ...rest] => match rc {
    (r, _) => match h {
      (hr, _) => // drift-audit: ignore — insertion into the small survivor list, not a loop accumulator
      if r > hr { [rc] ++ sorted } else { [h] ++ rank_insert(rest, rc) },
    },
  },
}

// Does `name`'s free-name closure reach `target`? collect_free_vars
// over-approximates the call edge (a reference without a call also
// excludes — the safe direction). `seen` breaks cycles.
fn reaches_decl(name: String, target: String, seen) with !Mutate = if target == "" {
  false
} else if name == target {
  true
} else if contains_name(seen, name) {
  false
} else {
  match fn_body_by_name(name) {
    None => false,
    Some(body) => // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by call-graph depth (the seen set), not a loop accumulator
    any((n) => reaches_decl(n, target, [name] ++ seen), collect_free_vars(body, [])),
  }
}

fn contains_name(names, target: String) = any((n) => n == target, names)

// A named fn's body node, read from the program weave directly — the
// same walk enclosing_fn_decl_at does, keyed by name instead of span.
// (The NModule-decls indirection read a projection the edit session's
// weave does not always carry — main() leaked past the reach exclusion
// because its body was unreachable through it; the weave is the one
// truth both walks read.)
fn fn_body_by_name(name: String) with !Mutate = {
  let snapshot = graph_snapshot()
  match snapshot {
    Graph(next, _spans) => fn_body_scan(name, 0, next),
  }
}

fn fn_body_scan(name: String, i, n) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  None
} else {
  match graph_node_body(i) {
    NStmt(FnStmt(fname, _params, _ret, _effs, body)) => if fname == name { Some(body) } else { fn_body_scan(name, i + 1, n) },
    _ => fn_body_scan(name, i + 1, n),
  }
}

// ─── Per-target-shape enumerators ──────────────────────────────────
//
// The graph already proves which bindings are constructors of this
// type: each typedef's `env_extend` registered its variants as
// ConstructorScheme entries whose scheme result type is
// TName(type_name, _). Read the live env snapshot and filter — never
// a second env-access effect (the EnvLookup re-derivation is dissolved).
fn enumerate_constructors(type_name, args, context) with !Mutate = match env_snapshot() {
  Env(entries) => ctors_of_type(entries, type_name, context),
}

// Filter live env bindings for this type's constructors, minting one
// candidate per constructor. A binding is a constructor of `type_name`
// when its kind is ConstructorScheme AND its scheme's result type is
// TName(type_name, _) (nullary ctors carry TName directly; fielded ctors
// carry TFun(_, TName(type_name, _), _)) — per infer.mn:1878-1881.
// Forward walk preserves the enrichment's effect order (oldest entry
// enriched first, exactly as the rest-before-head recursion ran);
// reverse restores the newest-first result order consumers saw.
fn ctors_of_type(entries, type_name, context) with !Mutate = {
  let n = len(entries)
  reverse(ctors_of_type_from(entries, type_name, 0, n, make_list(n), 0))
}

fn ctors_of_type_from(entries, type_name, i, n, own buf, count) with !Mutate = if i >= n {
  slice(buf, 0, count)
} else {
  match list_index(entries, i) {
    (name, scheme, reason, kind) => if is_ctor_of(kind, scheme, type_name) {
      ctors_of_type_from(
        entries,
        type_name,
        i + 1,
        n,
        list_set(buf, count, ctor_to_enriched_candidate(name, scheme, reason)),
        count + 1
      )
    } else {
      ctors_of_type_from(entries, type_name, i + 1, n, buf, count)
    },
  }
}

fn ctor_to_enriched_candidate(name, scheme, reason) with !Mutate = EnrichedCandidate({
  node: synthesize_ctor_call_node(name, scheme),
  origin: ProposerEnumerative,
  reason: VarLookup(name, reason),
  writes: [],
})

fn enumerate_lambdas(params, ret, row, context) = {
  // Single anonymous-fn candidate: (param_names) => ?? body. The
  // body becomes a nested Synth invocation; the chain re-enters
  // recursively at parse-time when the user accepts.
  let buf = make_list(1)
  let candidate = EnrichedCandidate({
    node: synthesize_lambda_node(params, ret),
    origin: ProposerEnumerative,
    reason: Inferred("an anonymous fn matching the hole's shape; its body is the next hole"),
    writes: [],
  })
  list_set(buf, 0, candidate)
  buf
}

// A refined target seeds from its DOMAIN rather than from the unrefined
// floor. The rule is boundary-value analysis and it carries NO width
// constant, which is the point: a threshold would be a number nobody can
// derive, and the bounds plus the interior value nearest zero are three
// deterministic reads that answer every shape.
//
//   `Int where 0 < self`        -> [1, ∞)   -> one seed, 1, and the two the
//                                             floor would have minted are
//                                             never constructed
//   `Int where 0 <= self <= 1`  -> [0, 1]   -> two seeds, 0 and 1, exactly
//                                             the inhabitants
//   `Int where self == 7`       -> {7}      -> one seed, and it is the only
//                                             value the type admits
//   an unreadable predicate     -> the floor, and the gate still judges
//
// A domain that admits NOTHING is a finding, not an empty list: the
// refinement is unsatisfiable and the medium should say so at the hole rather
// than propose silence. That projection is named residue
// (`Hβ.synth.empty-domain-teaches`), and until it lands the empty seed set
// falls through to the vocabulary, which is honest but quiet.
fn enumerate_domain(ty, allowed_effects, context) with !Mutate = {
  let d = ty_domain(ty)
  match d {
    DFinite(vs) => vs |> map(lit_candidate),
    DUndecided => enumerate_int_literals(context),
    DRange(lo, hi) => match (lo, hi) {
      (None, None) => enumerate_int_literals(context),
      _ => domain_bounds_seeds(lo, hi) |> map(int_literal_candidate),
    },
  }
}

// The bounds themselves, plus the interior value nearest zero — deduped, so a
// bound that IS that value yields one seed rather than two of it.
fn domain_bounds_seeds(lo, hi) = {
  let ends = [] |> push_opt(lo) |> push_opt(hi)
  match interior_near_zero(lo, hi) {
    Some(z) => if ends |> any(int_same(z, ??)) { ends } else { ends ++ [z] },
    None => ends,
  }
}

fn push_opt(v, xs) = match v {
  Some(n) => xs ++ [n],
  None => xs,
}

fn int_same(a, b) with Pure = a == b

// Zero when the range admits it; otherwise the open end has no interior to
// name and the closed end already seeded it.
fn interior_near_zero(lo, hi) =
  if bound_admits_lo(lo, 0) && bound_admits_hi(hi, 0) { Some(0) } else { None }

fn lit_candidate(v) with !Mutate = match v {
  LVInt(n) => int_literal_candidate(n),
  LVFloat(f) => float_literal_candidate(f),
  LVString(s) => EnrichedCandidate({
    node: synthesize_string_literal_node(s),
    origin: ProposerEnumerative,
    reason: Inferred("the type's string inhabitants"),
    writes: [],
  }),
  LVBool(b) => EnrichedCandidate({
    node: synth_node(LitBool(b)),
    origin: ProposerEnumerative,
    reason: Inferred("the type's boolean inhabitants"),
    writes: [],
  }),
}

fn enumerate_int_literals(context) = {
  // Literal seeds: 0, 1, -1. Three candidates per the Numerical
  // Survey discipline (least-trivial inhabitants of TInt).
  let buf = make_list(3)
  list_set(buf, 0, int_literal_candidate(0))
  list_set(buf, 1, int_literal_candidate(1))
  list_set(buf, 2, int_literal_candidate(0 - 1))
  buf
}

fn int_literal_candidate(n) with !Mutate = EnrichedCandidate({
  node: synthesize_int_literal_node(n),
  origin: ProposerEnumerative,
  reason: Inferred("the type's integer inhabitants"),
  writes: [],
})

fn enumerate_float_literals(context) = {
  let buf = make_list(2)
  list_set(buf, 0, float_literal_candidate(0.0))
  list_set(buf, 1, float_literal_candidate(1.0))
  buf
}

fn float_literal_candidate(f) with !Mutate = EnrichedCandidate({
  node: synthesize_float_literal_node(f),
  origin: ProposerEnumerative,
  reason: Inferred("the type's float inhabitants"),
  writes: [],
})

fn enumerate_string_literals(context) = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({
      node: synthesize_string_literal_node(""),
      origin: ProposerEnumerative,
      reason: Inferred("the type's string inhabitants"),
      writes: [],
    })
  )
  buf
}

fn one_unit_candidate(context) = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({
      node: synthesize_unit_literal_node(),
      origin: ProposerEnumerative,
      reason: Inferred("the unit type's one inhabitant"),
      writes: [],
    })
  )
  buf
}

fn one_hole_candidate(context) = {
  // ?? at unbound positions. The recursive case: hole-fills-with-
  // hole, leaving the gradient to narrow further at the next layer.
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({
      node: synthesize_hole_node(),
      origin: ProposerEnumerative,
      reason: Inferred("a nested ?? — the shape narrows, the fill recurses"),
      writes: [],
    })
  )
  buf
}

fn one_continuation_candidate(context) = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({
      node: synthesize_resume_node(),
      origin: ProposerEnumerative,
      reason: Inferred("the continuation's own resume(...)"),
      writes: [],
    })
  )
  buf
}

fn enumerate_records(fields, context) = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({
      node: synthesize_record_node(fields),
      origin: ProposerEnumerative,
      reason: Inferred("the type's record construction"),
      writes: [],
    })
  )
  buf
}

fn enumerate_list_candidates(elem, context) = {
  // Two candidates: empty list, single-element with elem-hole.
  let buf = make_list(2)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({
      node: synthesize_empty_list_node(),
      origin: ProposerEnumerative,
      reason: Inferred("the type's list inhabitants — the empty list"),
      writes: [],
    })
  )
  list_set(
    buf,
    1,
    EnrichedCandidate({
      node: synthesize_singleton_list_node(elem),
      origin: ProposerEnumerative,
      reason: Inferred("the type's list inhabitants — one element, itself a hole"),
      writes: [],
    })
  )
  buf
}

fn enumerate_tuple_candidates(elems, context) = {
  let buf = make_list(1)
  list_set(
    buf,
    0,
    EnrichedCandidate({
      node: synthesize_tuple_node(elems),
      origin: ProposerEnumerative,
      reason: Inferred("the type's tuple construction — each element a hole"),
      writes: [],
    })
  )
  buf
}

// DONE-CRITERION (Programming by Navigation — Lubin, Ziegler & Chasins,
// PLDI 2025, defs 3.5–3.6, under the 2025-10-09 errata's covering
// reading): the proposer offers ONLY candidates that reach a valid
// completion (Strong Soundness — the proof gate) and cuts off NO
// reachable completion (Strong Completeness — a covering condition,
// never step enumeration), SCOPED to the decidable fragment
// (census-clean infer + ground-decidable Verify; V_Pending debt sits
// outside the guarantee — for undecidable validity the pair is provably
// impossible, op. cit. §8). An EMPTY fan is Fail-Fast's theorem: no
// valid completion exists from here — a teachable refusal, never a
// shrug.
// ─── THE SEGMENT WALK — candidates judged on the ONE live graph ──────
//
// Every candidate is judged inside the fork TRIPLE — checkpoint, world top,
// judge, rollback, restore — over the graph the cursor is already sitting
// in. The copy this replaces spawned each candidate as a task against a
// FRESH graph instance seeded from four root views inside a pre-planned
// `n × (2048 + 64)`-handle band, and the measurement that condemned it is
// that its in-flight window was 1: one task spawned per block and joined
// immediately, so the isolation machinery was protecting against a
// concurrency that was switched off. It bought nothing and cost four
// things — a fact proven in one branch was invisible to the next, the
// hole's own cell was unreachable, the image paid ~10.5k planned handles
// per five-survivor propose, and the trail recorded the binds in an
// instance no diff could read.
//
// THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. The world is restored before the facts are
// replayed, because the replay re-performs `env_extend` and `diag_report`
// and those must resolve to the ROOT chain. The rollback comes first for
// the same reason: a replay must not run while the candidate's own graph
// writes are still live.
//
// The candidates are minted BEFORE any checkpoint (the arm reads the mint
// frontier at
// the arm's head, ahead of enumeration), so `graph_rollback`'s MFreshNode
// shrink never un-allocates the node being judged.
fn segment_verify(candidates, hole_h, below) = if len(candidates) == 0 {
  []
} else {
  let base = env_base_view()
  let seed = consume_base()
  let iseed = intern_seed()
  // THE FAN IS A FANOUT (C9, PLAN §11.1): the candidates fan through their
  // judgment as independent branches, and the width is whatever schedule
  // governs the propose — none installed, so the walk is sequential by
  // PROPERTY, never by a constant. A `~> parallel_compose` over a propose
  // is refused where it stands: each branch writes the one graph through
  // `graph_handler`, a stateful handler beyond this frame, which is the
  // race rule's own verdict and 9.2's deterministic partition stated as a
  // gate (`Hβ.lower.schedule-specialized-callee`).
  candidates |> fanout(judge_in_segment(hole_h, base, seed, iseed, below))
}

// Judges one candidate inside its own trail segment and joins its facts to the
// root only if it proved.
//
// A REFUTED BRANCH'S FACTS DO NOT REACH THE ROOT, and that changed here.
// The copy-era join replayed every branch "proven or not", which was benign
// only while a structural pre-filter guaranteed no candidate could fail to
// type. With the hole a real cell, a refused candidate reports an ordinary
// type mismatch — and that mismatch is the SEARCH discovering a dead
// branch, never the developer's program being wrong. Replaying it would
// make the proposer print errors about code nobody wrote. So the join is
// the proven branch's alone; the refutation survives as the verdict.
fn judge_in_segment(hole_h, base, seed, iseed, below, candidate) = {
  let cp = graph_push_checkpoint()
  let w = world_top()
  let outcome = segment_judge(
    candidate_node(candidate),
    hole_h,
    candidate_reason(candidate),
    base,
    seed,
    iseed,
    cp,
    below
  )
  graph_rollback(cp)
  world_restore(w)
  match outcome {
    (proven, inner, writes) => if proven {
      branch_join(inner)
      SegmentVerdict({proven: true, reason: candidate_reason(candidate), writes: writes})
    } else {
      SegmentVerdict({proven: false, reason: refusal_reason(inner), writes: []})
    },
  }
}

// Renders why a segment refused its candidate, from the medium's own
// `diag_message` rather than the banked line.
//
// A REFUSAL KEEPS ITS WHY, exactly as a row refusal always has. The branch
// is rolled back and its diagnostics never reach the root, so without this
// the medium would drop a candidate in silence — and a candidate the
// developer cannot see refused is a guess they cannot audit (the same
// sentence the row prune has carried since it was written). The medium's
// OWN rendering of what went wrong is the reason; nothing is invented here.
fn refusal_reason(inner) with !Mutate = match inner {
  (_, dfacts, _, _) => if len(dfacts) == 0 {
    Inferred("the hole's refinement is not satisfied")
  } else {
    // The DIAGNOSTIC's own message, read from the structural DiagKind the
    // bank carries beside the rendered line. The rendered line is the wrong
    // source here: it is stamped with a module and span belonging to
    // wherever the judgment happened to be standing, so a candidate refused
    // at this hole would cite a coordinate in a file the developer never
    // opened. The message is the part that is about the candidate.
    match dfacts[0] {
      (diag, _, _) => Inferred(diag_message(diag)),
    }
  },
}

// The candidate's judgment under the segment bracket — the branch chain
// minus its graph copy. The facts are read INSIDE, where the per-segment
// ledgers are still installed, and replayed by the caller once the graph
// and world are the root's again.
//
// The diagnostic fact list is the TYPE-ADMISSION verdict, not merely
// baggage for the replay: a candidate whose cell refuses the hole's says so
// through the same report path every other expression uses, so an empty
// list IS "this candidate unified". Reading the medium's own voice beats
// any comparison standing beside it.
fn segment_judge(node, hole_h, reason, base, seed, iseed, cp, below) = {
  let body = () => {
    consume_seed(seed)
    let admitted = candidate_proven(node, hole_h, reason)
    let vdebt = verify_debt()
    let clog = consume_log()
    let pubs = env_publishes()
    let dfacts = diag_lines_taken()
    // The context cells this candidate bound, read while the binds are
    // still live — the rollback that follows is exactly why the segment
    // carries them out as facts (C6).
    let writes = context_writes(cp, below)
    (admitted && len(dfacts) == 0, (pubs, dfacts, vdebt, clog), writes)
  }
  segment_bracket(body, base, iseed)
}

// Each moved context cell with what it now means: a type chased deep (an
// in-segment variable left inside it is a fresh cell of this segment,
// which the comparison reads up to renaming), a row resolved. A cell the
// bind left FREE, or a record-row residual, carries no fact to compare.
fn context_writes(cp, below) = graph_written_since(cp, below) |> fold([], context_write_step)

fn context_write_step(acc, h) = match context_fact(h) {
  Some(f) => push(acc, (h, f)),
  None => acc,
}

fn context_fact(h) = match graph_chase(h) {
  GNode(kind, _) => match kind {
    NBound(ty) => Some(FactTy(chase_deep(ty))),
    NRowBound(row) => Some(FactRow(resolve_row(row))),
    NFree(_) => None,
    NRowFree(_) => None,
    NRecordRowBound(_, _) => None,
    NErrorHole(_) => None,
  },
}

// The proven candidates, each SEALED with its segment's context writes —
// the one place a candidate's record learns what its judgment taught.
fn keep_survivors(candidates, verdicts) =
  candidates |> enumerate |> filter(verdict_at(verdicts)) |> map(sealed_value(verdicts))

fn verdict_at(verdicts, indexed) = match indexed {
  (i, _) => match verdicts[i] {
    SegmentVerdict(v) => v.proven,
  },
}

fn sealed_value(verdicts, indexed) = match indexed {
  (i, cand) => match verdicts[i] {
    SegmentVerdict(v) => with_writes(cand, v.writes),
  },
}

fn with_writes(candidate, writes) = match candidate {
  EnrichedCandidate(c) => EnrichedCandidate({node: c.node, origin: c.origin, reason: c.reason, writes: writes}),
}

// The NAMED candidates a segment refused, each with the medium's own words
// for why. A skeleton the enumerator built has no name to teach with —
// callee_name_of answers None — so only vocabulary refusals surface, which
// is the honest line: the developer can act on "empty() was refused
// because …" and cannot act on "the one-element list shape was refused".
fn named_refusals(candidates, verdicts) =
  candidates |> enumerate |> fold([], keep_named_refusal(verdicts))

fn keep_named_refusal(verdicts, acc, indexed) = match indexed {
  (i, cand) => match verdicts[i] {
    SegmentVerdict(v) => if v.proven {
      acc
    } else {
      match candidate_head_name(candidate_node(cand)) {
        Some(name) => // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by the candidate set, not a loop accumulator
        acc ++ [(name, v.reason)],
        None => acc,
      }
    },
  },
}

// Decides whether a candidate inhabits the hole: infer the candidate, `unify`
// its cell with the hole's own, then discharge every contract claimed of the
// hole against the candidate. The cell holds the shape and the claims hold
// the refinements (P0) — the cell used to be read for both, and once it held
// a shape every integer seed survived a `0 < self` return.
//
// THE PROOF GATE, one home, and the hole is a TERM CELL in it.
//
// §1's law is that any intelligence may propose and nothing executes
// unproven, so what "proven" MEANS has exactly one definition. It used to
// instantiate the target FRESH — `instantiate(Frozen(free_in_ty(t), t))` —
// and its comment called that "so the proof never binds the hole's live
// frontier", which reads as a safeguard and was a description of the
// defect: with a fresh copy there is no cell to bind, so the candidate was
// never checked against the hole at all, only against a type-shaped
// rumour of it. A `??` is a cell in the same union-find as every type
// variable (PLAN §11.1), and this is where that stops being a design
// sentence: infer the candidate, UNIFY its cell with the hole's, and let
// the one machine answer. Safe by construction — the caller's segment
// brackets this in graph_push_checkpoint + heap_mark + world_top and rolls
// it back, so a bind made here is undone before the next candidate.
//
// The unify carries the CANDIDATE'S OWN Reason rather than a minted one:
// the bind dies at rollback, so a fresh Reason constructor would have zero
// readers, and the candidate already knows why it was proposed.
//
// PROVEN is now three conjuncts, and the first is new: the judgment raised
// NOTHING. A candidate whose cell refuses the hole's reports a type
// mismatch through the ordinary diagnostic path, so "no diagnostics" is
// the type-admission verdict — read from the medium's own voice instead of
// re-derived by a comparison beside it.
fn candidate_proven(node, hole_h, reason) = {
  {
    infer_expr(node)
  } ~> row_gate ~> infer_ctx ~> fresh_for_inference ~> resume_summaries_ctx
  match node {
    N(_, span, h) => {
      unify(hole_h, h, span, reason)
      verify_after_apply(h) && all(refinement_admits(??, h), graph_claims_of(hole_h))
    },
  }
}

// ─── Verification — every candidate through the one gate ────────────
//
// The kernel's proof gate: each candidate is judged in its own trail
// segment over the ONE live graph, and only proven survivors reach the
// user. (This block said "fanned: candidates verify as spawned branch
// cursors" and named a fn deleted when the copy went — prose describing
// machinery that no longer exists is the Carried-Truth Law at the comment
// layer, and it is corrected here rather than dated.)
// THE FORK/MERGE COMPOSITION, at its one seam (PLAN §5's optimality
// half + the duality): the segment walk has just explored MEANING space by
// FORKING — candidates conflict, so each proved in its own rolled-back
// world, and proof is a FILTER (admit / refuse). Every survivor is then
// an equality CLASS, not one program, so the second half explores
// FORM-space by MERGING — saturation over the candidates' own range
// draws the equivalence edges, extraction picks the cost-minimal member,
// and nothing is ever undone because equals compose. The order is
// FORCED: extract-then-prove would optimize a program that may be
// inadmissible. What reaches the user is therefore proven AND
// extraction-optimal, never merely proven.
// The declared row lost `WasiThreads` and `BranchDiag` at the segment walk,
// and that loss IS the measurement: nothing spawns a task any more, so the
// judgment no longer reaches the threading vocabulary at all. The medium
// named it (`T_OverDeclared`) the moment the spawn left.
fn verify_each_enriched(candidates, hole_h, cand_start) with !Mutate = {
  let verdicts = segment_verify(candidates, hole_h, cand_start)
  let refusals = named_refusals(candidates, verdicts)
  let survivors = keep_survivors(candidates, verdicts)
  // The form half — only over the range this propose minted (it keeps a
  // cursor-position fan from re-saturating the whole session graph).
  saturate_range(cand_start, graph_next())
  (survivors |> map(extract_optimal) |> collapse_equivalent, refusals)
}

// THE FALSE TIE DISSOLVES (the duality's sharpest immediate
// consequence). §1's law is that two proven survivors cannot be picked
// between — they MEAN different things and only the author knows which,
// so the medium asks. But two survivors that extract to the SAME
// e-class do NOT mean different things: they are one meaning in two
// forms, and the tie between them is a question with no content. After
// extraction they collapse to one proposal, so the teaching tie-break
// fires only on a REAL disagreement of meaning. Keyed on the extracted
// handle — the e-class root the canon weave already carries, never a
// re-derived structural compare.
fn collapse_equivalent(cands) = fold([], collapse_step, cands)

fn collapse_step(acc, c) = if any(same_node_as(c), acc) { acc } else { acc ++ [c] }

fn same_node_as(c, k) with Pure = node_handle(candidate_node(k)) == node_handle(candidate_node(c))

fn candidate_node(candidate) with Pure = match candidate {
  EnrichedCandidate(c) => c.node,
}

// The candidate's own WHY, read where the bind needs one. The segment's
// unify is rolled back, so minting a Reason for it would be a constructor
// with no reader; the candidate already carries the answer.
fn candidate_reason(candidate) with Pure = match candidate {
  EnrichedCandidate(c) => c.reason,
}

// The NAME a refusal can teach with: the head of a call whatever its
// arity, or a bare name. callee_name_of answers only for the zero-arg
// form, which is right for its own reader (a vocabulary call) and wrong
// here — a refused `MkBox(…)` is exactly the kind of refusal a developer
// needs to see, and it has an arg. A skeleton with no head name teaches
// nothing and stays out.
fn candidate_head_name(node) = match node {
  N(body, _, _) => match body {
    NExpr(CallExpr(callee, _)) => match callee {
      N(cb, _, _) => match cb {
        NExpr(VarRef(name)) => Some(name),
        _ => None,
      },
    },
    NExpr(VarRef(name)) => Some(name),
    _ => None,
  },
}

// A survivor's node, replaced by the cost-minimal member of its own
// e-class. The extraction is a live chase of the canon weave (the same
// union-find the type layer uses); an unmoved handle keeps the node it
// already had, so a candidate with no cheaper equivalent pays nothing.
fn extract_optimal(candidate) = match candidate {
  EnrichedCandidate(c) => {
    let h = node_handle(c.node)
    let cheapest = egraph_extract(h)
    if cheapest == h {
      candidate
    } else {
      // The CHEAPER BODY at the CANDIDATE'S OWN SPAN: a proposal is
      // about the position the developer is filling, so taking the
      // rewritten node's span would answer a `why` with the coordinates
      // of a node they never wrote (caught in review of this fn's first
      // form). The rewrite's own justification belongs on the canon
      // EDGE — the one kernel write that carries no Reason today
      // (Hβ.egraph.canon-edge-carries-reason); until it does, the
      // candidate's seed Reason is the honest chain.
      EnrichedCandidate({
        node: N(graph_node_body(cheapest), node_span(c.node), cheapest),
        origin: c.origin,
        reason: c.reason,
        writes: c.writes,
      })
    }
  },
}

fn node_span(node) with Pure = match node {
  N(_, span, _) => span,
}

// ─── map_to_proposals — project each survivor to its (node, reason) ──
//    pair for the Synth effect's proposal contract. The seed Reason lives
//    in the candidate record — the node's own GNode reason is only its
//    mint-span record — so dropping the pair dropped the Why from every
//    rendered survivor.
// REFERENCE BEFORE MINT (SYNTAX §«Function literals»): at a function-typed
// hole the empty lambda skeleton is the medium saying "write one", and it is
// offered only when no function already in scope is proven there. It used to
// stand beside every reference, so `5 |> ??` with one fitting function in
// scope could never fill — the skeleton always tied with it.
fn reference_before_mint(survivors) = if any(is_reference_candidate, survivors) {
  filter(is_not_lambda_candidate, survivors)
} else {
  survivors
}

fn is_reference_candidate(c) with Pure = match c {
  EnrichedCandidate(cr) => match cr.node {
    N(body, _, _) => match body {
      NExpr(VarRef(_)) => true,
      _ => false,
    },
  },
}

fn is_not_lambda_candidate(c) with Pure = match c {
  EnrichedCandidate(cr) => match cr.node {
    N(body, _, _) => match body {
      NExpr(LambdaExpr(_, _)) => false,
      _ => true,
    },
  },
}

fn map_to_proposals(enriched) = map({ EnrichedCandidate(c) => (c.node, c.reason) }, enriched)

// ─── The verdict — computed ONCE, where the survivors are known ──────
//
// Five surfaces used to re-derive it from `len(survivors)`, and the copies
// disagreed: the address render dropped the lone survivor's Reason while
// keeping every tie's, so the medium showed its reasoning exactly when it
// was unsure and withheld it exactly when the developer was most likely to
// take the fill on faith. `VFill` holds the Reason because the constructor
// admits no other shape.
fn verdict_of(survivors, target_ty, context) = match distinct_by_canon(survivors, []) {
  [] => VNone,
  [only] => VFill(candidate_node(only), candidate_reason(only)),
  distinct => VAsk(divergence_of(distinct, target_ty, context), map_to_proposals(distinct)),
}

// SAME DENOTATION BY CANONICAL ROOT (C5): two survivors the e-graph has
// joined are one meaning in two forms, and a form tie is free (PLAN §5's
// duality) — the first spelling stands and the question is never asked
// about it. This is the e-graph's one role in the proposal: to DECIDE
// same-denotation cheaply, by the canon column the medium already keeps.
// It moves nothing today — every enumerator mints an atom and every rule
// matches a binary shape, so a survivor's root is itself
// (`Hβ.synth.fan-extraction-needs-a-feeder`) — and it is the read the
// first fusing rule will fall through.
fn distinct_by_canon(cands, acc) = if len(cands) == 0 {
  acc
} else {
  let root = egraph_extract(node_handle(candidate_node(cands[0])))
  if any({ c => egraph_extract(node_handle(candidate_node(c))) == root }, acc) {
    distinct_by_canon(rest(cands), acc)
  } else {
    distinct_by_canon(rest(cands), push(acc, cands[0]))
  }
}

// WHERE the survivors diverge — the trail first, then the term (C6).
fn divergence_of(survivors, target_ty, context) = match first_context_split(survivors) {
  Some(d) => d,
  None => divergence_of_terms(map(candidate_node, survivors), target_ty, context),
}

// THE TRAIL FIRST: the earliest context cell the top survivor's segment
// bound that another survivor's segment bound to something else — in the
// top survivor's own write order, which is the order its judgment reached
// the context. A survivor that never wrote the cell is read at the cell's
// live value now (the pre-segment meaning every rollback restored); a cell
// one of them left free is no divergence, since a candidate that constrains
// the position less asks nothing of the developer.
fn first_context_split(survivors) = match survivors {
  [] => None,
  [top, ...others] => first_split_over(candidate_writes(top), others),
}

fn first_split_over(writes, others) = match writes {
  [] => None,
  [(h, fact), ...more] => match fold(None, first_some(split_against(h, fact)), others) {
    Some(d) => Some(d),
    None => first_split_over(more, others),
  },
}

fn first_some(f, acc, x) = match acc {
  Some(d) => Some(d),
  None => f(x),
}

// The divergence at cell `h` between the fact one survivor wrote and what
// another survivor's segment left there — None when they agree, when the
// other left the cell free, or when the two facts are of different sorts
// (a cell has one sort; a sort clash is no question for the developer).
fn split_against(h, fact, other) = match fact_at(other, h) {
  None => None,
  Some(theirs) => fact_split(h, fact, theirs),
}

fn fact_at(candidate, h) = match find({ (k, _) => k == h }, candidate_writes(candidate)) {
  Some(entry) => match entry {
    (_, f) => Some(f),
  },
  None => context_fact(h),
}

fn fact_split(h, mine, theirs) = match mine {
  FactTy(a) => match theirs {
    FactTy(b) => if ty_agree(a, b) { None } else { Some(DivType(a, b, graph_reason_at(h))) },
    FactRow(_) => None,
  },
  FactRow(a) => match theirs {
    FactRow(b) => if row_differs(a, b) { Some(DivRow(a, b)) } else { None },
    FactTy(_) => None,
  },
}

// Alpha-equivalence over the chased types: the variables a segment left
// inside a fact are its own fresh cells, comparable only up to renaming.
fn ty_agree(a, b) = match ty_alpha_eq(a, b, []) {
  (ok, _) => ok,
}

fn candidate_writes(candidate) with Pure = match candidate {
  EnrichedCandidate(c) => c.writes,
}

// THE TERM: no context cell separates the survivors, so the question is
// about what stands at the position, in the precedence types.mn's
// `Divergence` states — row, then denotation, then value, then shape.
// Every read is LIVE at question time — a callee's row from the very env
// entry the vocabulary enumerator admitted it through, a denotation from the
// callee's own body — so nothing rides on the candidate record and nothing
// can go stale between the enumeration and the question.
//
// The nodes arrive RANK-SORTED, so a row split is reported as the top
// survivor against the first one that may do something it may not.
fn divergence_of_terms(nodes, target_ty, context) = match first_row_split(context, nodes) {
  Some(split) => match split {
    (a, b) => DivRow(a, b),
  },
  None => match target_ty {
    TFun(_, _, _) => // At a function-typed hole the survivors are different COMPUTATIONS of
    // one type: no denotation read separates them, and "shape" was the
    // wrong word for two functions with the same shape.
    DivBehavior,
    _ => divergence_by_denotation(nodes, target_ty),
  },
}

fn divergence_by_denotation(nodes, target_ty) = {
  let denotations = map(candidate_denotation([]), nodes)
  if any(denotation_unread, denotations) {
    // A survivor that is not a constant at all makes this a choice
    // between structures, and the domain has nothing to say about it —
    // unless every survivor is a nullary constructor of the target: those
    // ARE the type's values, and the question is which one (C7: Circle
    // against Square, True against False, asked as shape before).
    match nullary_ctor_names(nodes) {
      Some(names) => DivVariants(names),
      None => DivShape,
    }
  } else if denotations_alike(denotations) {
    DivName
  } else {
    DivValue(ty_domain(target_ty))
  }
}

// Every survivor's constructor name when each is a bare nullary constructor
// reference — read from the env entry the reference resolves to — else None.
fn nullary_ctor_names(nodes) = fold(Some([]), nullary_ctor_step, nodes)

fn nullary_ctor_step(acc, node) = match acc {
  None => None,
  Some(names) => match nullary_ctor_name(node) {
    Some(name) => Some(push(names, name)),
    None => None,
  },
}

fn nullary_ctor_name(node) = match node {
  N(body, _, _) => match body {
    NExpr(VarRef(name)) => match env_lookup(name) {
      Some(entry) => match entry {
        (_, _, kind) => match kind {
          ConstructorScheme(_, _) => Some(name),
          _ => None,
        },
      },
      None => None,
    },
    _ => None,
  },
}

// Rows compare by MUTUAL subsumption — the crown's own algebra, never a
// structural compare, because two spellings of one row are one row.
fn first_row_split(context, nodes) = {
  let rows = map(candidate_row(context), nodes)
  let base = head(rows)
  match find(row_differs(base), rest(rows)) {
    Some(r) => Some((base, r)),
    None => None,
  }
}

fn row_differs(a, b) = !(row_subsumes(a, b) && row_subsumes(b, a))

// A literal, a constructor, a lambda skeleton performs nothing by itself,
// and Pure is that reading rather than a fallback. A call carries its
// callee's declared row.
fn candidate_row(context, node) = match callee_name_of(node) {
  None => mk_ef_pure(),
  Some(name) => scope_row_of(name, context),
}

// The callee's row, re-read from the scope the enumerator admitted this
// candidate through — one home, read live, never carried on the candidate
// record beside its rendered Reason text. The scan is by NAME and pays the
// O(1) law's standing debt with every other env read (`Hβ.perf.env-o1-index`,
// `Hβ.perf.name-is-handle`); it runs once per survivor of a tie.
fn scope_row_of(name: String, context) = match context {
  Context(scope_bindings, _, _, _) => match find(binding_is_named(name), scope_bindings) {
    Some(entry) => match entry {
      (_, scheme, _, _) => extract_row(judgment_ty(scheme)),
    },
    None => mk_ef_pure(),
  },
}

fn binding_is_named(name: String, entry) = match entry {
  (ename, _, _, _) => ename == name,
}

// What this candidate DENOTES, if the medium can read it: a literal denotes
// itself, and a zero-arg call denotes whatever its callee's body denotes,
// followed down the call chain (`seen` breaks a cycle the vocabulary's own
// nontermination exclusion does not reach, since that exclusion is about the
// HOLE's enclosing decl, not about the callees of a candidate).
//
// This read is why `7` and `pure_seven()` classify as one value with two
// names. It is the shape of the e-graph rewrite that would MERGE them — a
// Pure nullary fn inlined to its literal body — and when that rule lands the
// two collapse into one class and extraction answers with a fill; until
// then, asking which name carries the intent is the honest answer, not a
// stand-in for one (`Hβ.synth.fan-extraction-needs-a-feeder`).
fn candidate_denotation(seen, node) = match callee_name_of(node) {
  Some(name) => if contains_name(seen, name) {
    None
  } else {
    match fn_body_by_name(name) {
      None => None,
      Some(body) => // drift-audit: ignore — bounded by call-graph depth (the seen set), not a loop accumulator
      candidate_denotation([name] ++ seen, body),
    }
  },
  None => node_const(node_handle(node)),
}

fn denotation_unread(d) with Pure = match d {
  None => true,
  Some(_) => false,
}

// Every survivor reading as one constant. The comparison is asked about the
// LitValues THEMSELVES, never about the `Option` around them: a polymorphic
// sum's payload compares by ADDRESS today, so `Some(x) == Some(y)` answers
// false for structurally equal payloads — measured 2026-09-18, one
// $eq_nOption helper serving every instantiation because `fold_sig` folds
// `TName(n, args)` to the bare name and the declared payload type is the
// quantified var (`Hβ.eq.polymorphic-sum-payload-is-pointer-eq`,
// tests/frontier/mn-eq-polymorphic-sum.mn). LitValue's own payloads are
// ground — Int, Float, String, Bool — so this compare is the total
// structural one, and it stays right when that peer closes.
//
// The element pin is an Intent Boundary (the concat precedent above): the
// list arrives through `map`, whose element identity the graph cannot prove
// at the compare site, and the twin keys on what the author states once.
fn denotations_alike(ds: [Option(LitValue)]) = match head(ds) {
  None => false,
  Some(first) => all(denotes_value(first), rest(ds)),
}

// The pins are the Intent Boundary the comparison needs: `denotes_value` is
// partially applied into `all`, so the element identity the caller states
// does not reach the closure's own parameters, and an unpinned `==` here
// would fall to the word compare the unprovable-comparison census counts.
// The author states the identity once; both operands are then ground and the
// compare is the total structural one (PLAN's pointer-eq-on-names class, the
// same remedy its own entry names).
fn denotes_value(first: LitValue, d: Option(LitValue)) = match d {
  None => false,
  Some(v) => v == first,
}

// ─── Node synthesizers — per-shape AST construction ─────────────────
//
// Each helper produces a Node value the verify gate can apply to the
// graph. The actual construction reuses parser_infra's nexpr/nstmt
// constructors via the wheel's existing Node ADT (types.mn:376-379).
fn synthesize_int_literal_node(n) with !Mutate = synth_node(LitInt(n))

fn synthesize_float_literal_node(f) with !Mutate = synth_node(LitFloat(f))

fn synthesize_string_literal_node(s) with !Mutate = synth_node(LitString(s))

fn synthesize_unit_literal_node() with !Mutate = synth_node(LitUnit)

fn synthesize_hole_node() = synth_hole_node()

fn synthesize_resume_node() with !Mutate = synth_node(ResumeExpr(synth_unit_node(), []))

fn synthesize_empty_list_node() with !Mutate = synth_node(MakeListExpr([]))

fn synthesize_singleton_list_node(elem) with !Mutate = synth_node(MakeListExpr([synth_hole_node()]))

fn synthesize_tuple_node(elems) with !Mutate = synth_node(MakeTupleExpr(synth_holes_for(elems)))

fn synthesize_record_node(fields) with !Mutate =
  synth_node(MakeRecordExpr(synth_record_holes_for(fields)))

// Builds a constructor candidate: a bare `VarRef` when the scheme is nullary,
// a `CallExpr` with one hole per field when it is not.
//
// A NULLARY CONSTRUCTOR IS A VALUE, NOT A CALL — and this read is the fix
// for a defect that had always been here and could not be seen. The node
// was minted as `CallExpr(VarRef(name), holes)` unconditionally, so `None`
// became `None()`: a zero-arg application of something that is not a
// function, ill-typed by construction. It stayed invisible because the
// resulting mismatch leaked out of the candidate's own judgment and landed
// at a span in an unrelated module, while the candidate still rendered as a
// bare `??` — so the developer saw a foreign error and a proposal that was
// never admissible. The hole becoming a real cell is what surfaced it: with
// the candidate checked against the hole, the refusal became visible
// instead of ambient.
//
// The distinguishing fact is one the scheme already carries and the
// constructor filter already reads (nullary ctors carry TName directly;
// fielded ones carry TFun(_, TName, _)), so this is a read, never a new
// fact: SYNTAX's own surface says `let nothing = None`.
fn synthesize_ctor_call_node(name, scheme) with !Mutate = match judgment_ty(scheme) {
  TFun(params, _, _) => synth_node(CallExpr(synth_node(VarRef(name)), synth_holes_for(params))),
  _ => synth_node(VarRef(name)),
}

// params arrive as the scheme's own [TParam] — LambdaExpr's exact
// carrier; rebuilding name lists from them was the mismatch the
// converged judge caught (the param-product law reaching synth).
fn synthesize_lambda_node(params, ret) with !Mutate =
  synth_node(LambdaExpr(map(proposed_param, params), synth_hole_node()))

// A proposed lambda's parameter carries the NAME the hole's type gives it
// and a fresh cell for its type: the judgment binds the cell when the
// skeleton meets the hole, and the render stays bare — a lambda parameter
// takes no annotation on the page, so a skeleton carrying the target's
// ground types rendered `(_: String, _: Bool) => ??`, which no parse reads
// (measured at the proposal battery's birth, C7).
fn proposed_param(p) =
  mk_param(param_name(p), TVar(graph_fresh_ty(Inferred("a proposed lambda's parameter"))))

// ─── Synthesis primitives — structured Node construction ───────────
//
// Every candidate node is MINTED into the one graph at a real handle
// via mint_node (parser.mn) — the same AST-in-graph fabric the parser
// births every node through. The fabricated handle-0 form was the
// load-bearing gap: a candidate with no handle cannot be inferred or
// chased, so the proof gate verified a fixed meaningless node. Minting
// edge-links the body into the graph weave so the proof gate's
// infer_expr walk binds the candidate's own handle (Carried-Truth: the
// graph holds the node; the gate reads it live).
fn synth_node(expr_body) with !Mutate = mint_node(NExpr(expr_body), span_zero())

fn synth_unit_node() = synth_node(LitUnit)

fn synth_hole_node() with !Mutate = mint_node(NHole(0), span_zero())

fn synth_holes_for(elems) = if len(elems) == 0 {
  []
} else {
  let n = len(elems)
  let buf = make_list(n)
  synth_holes_loop(0, n, buf)
}

fn synth_holes_loop(i, n, buf) = if i >= n {
  buf
} else {
  list_set(buf, i, synth_hole_node())
  synth_holes_loop(i + 1, n, buf)
}

// A record literal's carrier is the parser's (name, value) pair list —
// bare holes lost the names (the converged judge's catch); each demanded
// field keeps its name with a hole as its value.
fn synth_record_holes_for(fields) = map({ (fname, _) => (fname, synth_hole_node()) }, fields)

// ─── Composition with cursor_default + delta_default ────────────────
//
// The full Phase μ chain composes here:
//
//   cursor_session(initial_caret)
//       ~> delta_default              -- inverse-direction gradient (Hμ.gradient-delta)
//       ~> synth_default              -- proposer chain (this file)
//       ~> mentl_default              -- Teach + Why tentacles
//       ~> interrogate_default        -- automated eight-fold (Hμ.eight-interrogation-loop)
//       ~> terminal_transport         -- the human surface (Hμ.cursor.transport)
//
// The Phase μ peer handles + cursor + the kernel = the full Mentl
// active surface. ULTIMATE FORM is the medium running this chain
// continuously while the developer types — the bus-compressor closed
// at the human boundary, with proposers exploring and the gradient
// narrowing in real time. (The oracle-queue cache re-enters as graph
